::ENG3_U03_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Councilors, a law that cannot be explained to the people will soon be suspected by the people. Speak plainly, not because the crowd is simple, but because justice should not hide behind fog. What is the author's purpose? {
=To persuade councilors to make laws clear and understandable.#Correct. The speaker urges plain speech about law.
~To teach the crowd to avoid justice.#This contradicts the passage.
~To describe weather in the city.#Fog is figurative.
~To argue that laws should be hidden.#The passage says justice should not hide.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Councilors, a law that cannot be explained to the people will soon be suspected by the people. Speak plainly, not because the crowd is simple, but because justice should not hide behind fog. Who is the audience? {
~Children learning weather terms.#No children are addressed.
=Councilors.#Correct. The passage begins by addressing councilors.
~Travelers at a gate.#Travelers are not mentioned.
~Sailors in a storm.#No sailors appear.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Councilors, a law that cannot be explained to the people will soon be suspected by the people. Speak plainly, not because the crowd is simple, but because justice should not hide behind fog. Which choice best helps achieve purpose? {
~The author avoids all direct advice.#The passage directly advises "Speak plainly."
~The author praises confusing language.#The passage criticizes hidden justice.
=The author uses the image of fog to show unclear law as a problem.#Correct. The image supports the purpose by making confusion seem dangerous.
~The author changes the topic to farming.#No farming appears.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Councilors, a law that cannot be explained to the people will soon be suspected by the people. Speak plainly, not because the crowd is simple, but because justice should not hide behind fog. Which statement is only the topic, not the purpose? {
~To persuade councilors to speak plainly.#This is a purpose.
~To warn leaders that unclear laws create mistrust.#This is a purpose.
~To encourage clear public explanation of law.#This is a purpose.
=Law.#Correct. "Law" is only a topic.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: A teacher of youth should not pour answers as water into jars. Ask the learner to lift the cup, test the stream, and know why it is clean. What is the author's purpose? {
~To describe storing water.#The water image is metaphorical.
=To encourage teachers to guide students toward active understanding.#Correct. The passage argues for active learning.
~To persuade students never to ask questions.#The passage supports questioning and testing.
~To explain how to build jars.#The jars are figurative.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: A teacher of youth should not pour answers as water into jars. Ask the learner to lift the cup, test the stream, and know why it is clean. Which author choice helps achieve purpose? {
~The author lists unrelated city offices.#No offices appear.
~The author gives no image for learning.#The water and jar image is central.
~The author tells teachers to silence learners.#The passage encourages active learner action.
=The author compares passive learning to filling jars and active learning to testing a stream.#Correct. The comparison supports the purpose.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A teacher of youth should not pour answers as water into jars. Ask the learner to lift the cup, test the stream, and know why it is clean. What central idea supports the purpose? {
=True learning requires active understanding, not passive receiving.#Correct. This idea supports the author's advice to teachers.
~Students are containers and nothing more.#The jar image criticizes passive teaching.
~Water is always cleaner than speech.#The image is figurative.
~Teachers should avoid all instruction.#The passage calls for guided active learning.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Citizens, do not praise the wall and forget the neighbor outside it. A city that guards stone but neglects mercy has protected its shape and lost its soul. What is the author's purpose? {
~To give construction advice about walls.#The wall is used to discuss civic values.
=To warn citizens that security without mercy damages the city.#Correct. The author wants citizens to value mercy as well as protection.
~To argue that neighbors are unimportant.#The passage says not to forget the neighbor.
~To explain the geometry of cities.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Citizens, do not praise the wall and forget the neighbor outside it. A city that guards stone but neglects mercy has protected its shape and lost its soul. Which phrase best shows purpose? {
~"the wall"#This is an image, not the clearest purpose clue.
~"stone"#This supports the image.
="do not praise the wall and forget the neighbor"#Correct. This direct command shows what the author wants citizens to avoid.
~"its shape"#This is part of the contrast.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Citizens, do not praise the wall and forget the neighbor outside it. A city that guards stone but neglects mercy has protected its shape and lost its soul. How does word choice help achieve purpose? {
=The words "shape" and "soul" contrast outward safety with inner moral loss.#Correct. The contrast supports the warning about mercy.
~The word "wall" proves the passage is only about masonry.#This is too literal.
~The word "citizens" hides the audience.#It identifies the audience.
~The word "neighbor" shows mercy is irrelevant.#It makes mercy concrete.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Judges, anger is a swift horse, but justice must not ride it. Let evidence hold the reins. What is the author's purpose? {
~To teach judges how to ride horses.#The horse is metaphorical.
=To advise judges to let evidence guide decisions instead of anger.#Correct. The speaker warns judges against anger.
~To persuade judges to ignore evidence.#The passage says evidence should hold the reins.
~To praise anger as justice.#The passage separates anger from justice.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Judges, anger is a swift horse, but justice must not ride it. Let evidence hold the reins. Which author choice helps achieve purpose? {
=A metaphor comparing anger to a swift horse.#Correct. The metaphor makes anger seem powerful but dangerous.
~A list of court dates.#No dates are listed.
~A recipe for feeding horses.#Unsupported.
~A comparison of two cities.#No cities are compared.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Judges, anger is a swift horse, but justice must not ride it. Let evidence hold the reins. Which answer best connects audience and purpose? {
~The audience is farmers, and the purpose is to buy horses.#Unsupported.
=The audience is judges, and the purpose is to warn them to decide by evidence rather than anger.#Correct. This names audience and purpose accurately.
~The audience is horses, and the purpose is to teach law.#This misreads the metaphor.
~The audience is unknown, and the purpose is unclear.#The passage directly addresses judges.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: A ruler who eats first in famine teaches the people that power is hunger with a crown. Let the leader's table be last, and trust will arrive before bread. What is the author's purpose? {
~To explain royal cooking methods.#The table image supports leadership ethics.
~To argue that leaders should ignore famine.#The passage says leaders should serve last.
=To persuade rulers to sacrifice comfort and serve the people first.#Correct. The passage directs leaders toward servant leadership.
~To praise selfish power.#The passage criticizes it.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: A ruler who eats first in famine teaches the people that power is hunger with a crown. Let the leader's table be last, and trust will arrive before bread. Which phrase most clearly reveals purpose? {
~"hunger with a crown"#This criticizes selfish power but is less direct than the command.
="Let the leader's table be last"#Correct. This command shows what the author wants rulers to do.
~"arrive before bread"#This is figurative support.
~"in famine"#This provides the situation.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: A ruler who eats first in famine teaches the people that power is hunger with a crown. Let the leader's table be last, and trust will arrive before bread. What central idea supports the purpose? {
=Trust grows when leaders put public need before personal comfort.#Correct. This idea supports the call for leaders to serve last.
~Rulers should eat first to show power.#This contradicts the passage.
~Famine has no effect on leadership.#The situation is central.
~Bread is more important than trust in every case.#The passage says trust will arrive before bread.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Speakers, do not sharpen language only to wound. A true argument cuts away confusion so that understanding can stand. What is the author's purpose? {
~To encourage speakers to insult opponents.#The passage warns against wounding.
=To advise speakers to use argument for clarity rather than harm.#Correct. The author distinguishes harmful language from useful argument.
~To explain knife-making.#Sharpen and cuts are figurative.
~To reject all argument.#The passage values true argument.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Speakers, do not sharpen language only to wound. A true argument cuts away confusion so that understanding can stand. Which author choice helps achieve purpose? {
=The author uses blade imagery to contrast harm with clarity.#Correct. The imagery helps show the right and wrong use of argument.
~The author lists unrelated tools.#No list appears.
~The author hides the audience.#The passage addresses speakers.
~The author avoids figurative language.#The passage uses figurative language.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Speakers, do not sharpen language only to wound. A true argument cuts away confusion so that understanding can stand. What is the audience? {
~Judges only.#Judges are not addressed.
~Farmers only.#Farmers are not addressed.
=Speakers.#Correct. The passage directly addresses speakers.
~Children at play.#Unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Let no citizen say, "The square is not mine." A city dies when good people treat public space as another person's house. Sweep it, defend it, improve it, and it becomes yours by care. What is the author's purpose? {
=To persuade citizens to take responsibility for public spaces.#Correct. The author urges citizens to care for the square.
~To sell private houses.#The passage is about public space.
~To explain why cities must die.#The passage warns against neglect.
~To discourage civic care.#The passage urges care.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Let no citizen say, "The square is not mine." A city dies when good people treat public space as another person's house. Sweep it, defend it, improve it, and it becomes yours by care. Which choice helps the author achieve purpose? {
=The commands "Sweep it, defend it, improve it."#Correct. These commands show specific civic actions.
~The phrase "another person's house" proves the topic is real estate.#This is a comparison.
~The passage avoids action words.#It uses several action words.
~The passage never addresses citizens.#It begins by addressing citizens.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Let no citizen say, "The square is not mine." A city dies when good people treat public space as another person's house. Sweep it, defend it, improve it, and it becomes yours by care. Which purpose statement is strongest? {
~The author writes about a square.#This is topic, not purpose.
~The author uses words about houses.#This identifies wording but not purpose.
=The author urges citizens to see public spaces as shared responsibilities.#Correct. This states what the author wants the audience to do.
~The author describes sweeping only.#Sweeping is one example.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: If peace is desired, prepare justice before swords. A treaty signed by frightened hands will tear when fear changes direction. What is the author's purpose? {
~To argue that fear creates lasting peace.#The passage says fear-based treaties tear.
~To give instructions for sword storage.#Swords are part of a political image.
~To claim treaties are never useful.#The passage critiques treaties based only on fear.
=To persuade leaders that lasting peace depends on justice, not fear.#Correct. The passage contrasts justice with fear.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: If peace is desired, prepare justice before swords. A treaty signed by frightened hands will tear when fear changes direction. Which phrase helps achieve purpose through imagery? {
~"peace is desired"#This states the situation.
~"before swords"#This supports the contrast but is less vivid than the tearing treaty.
="will tear when fear changes direction"#Correct. This image shows why fear-based peace is unstable.
~"If peace"#This is an opening condition.
}

::ENG3_U03_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: If peace is desired, prepare justice before swords. A treaty signed by frightened hands will tear when fear changes direction. Which answer avoids confusing central idea with purpose? {
~The topic is peace, so the purpose is peace.#This confuses topic with purpose.
~The central idea is justice, so the purpose is justice.#This is incomplete.
=The central idea is that justice creates stable peace, and the purpose is to persuade leaders to prioritize justice before force.#Correct. This separates central idea from purpose.
~The purpose is to mention treaties.#This is not a purpose.
}
